Test blocks inside a test block's callback are listed one level deeper, each with its own line range from its leading annotations, so an agent can read one test of a long `describe`.
- Nesting is listed down to depth 2: the `it` inside `it('renders')` isn't listed.
- Hooks (`beforeEach`) and other calls aren't test blocks, and a test block in a callback without a block body (`() => it(...)`), or inside an expression such as a `const`, isn't listed.
- Table tests (`describe.each(table)('step %i', fn)`) list the blocks in their callback too.
== args
input/tutorial.test.ts
== file input/tutorial.test.ts
import { describe, it } from 'vitest';

describe('tutorial navigation', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {});

  /** The first step opens on load. */
  it('opens the first step', () => {
    expect(1).toBe(1);
  });

  describe.each([1, 2])('step %i', (n) => {
    it('renders', function () {
      it('too deep', () => {});
    });
  });

  test.skip(`skipped`, async () => {});
  const helper = () => it('in an expression');
});

it('top', () => it('expression body'));
== stdout
input/tutorial.test.ts (21 lines)
[L3-L19] describe('tutorial navigation')
  [L6-L9] it('opens the first step')
  [L11-L15] describe.each('step %i')
    [L12-L14] it('renders')
  [L17-L17] test.skip(`skipped`)
[L21-L21] it('top')
